1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,525
Chapter 44: Management of Obstetrical Hemorrhage.

3
00:00:06,525 --> 00:00:17,687
Management Of Hemorrhage Blood Replacement Obstetrical Coagulopathies Surgical Management Of Hemorrhage References Recognition of obstetrical hemorrhage severity is crucial to its management.

4
00:00:17,687 --> 00:00:24,412
However, visual estimates, especially when blood losses are excessive, are notoriously inaccurate.

5
00:00:24,412 --> 00:00:30,500
In many cases, true volume losses are often two to three times the clinical estimate.

6
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:36,675
Moreover, in obstetrics, part and sometimes even all of the lost blood may be concealed.

7
00:00:36,675 --> 00:00:43,812
Estimation is further complicated in that peripartum hemorrhage also includes the pregnancyaugmented blood volume.

8
00:00:43,812 --> 00:00:54,762
After pregnancy hypervolemia is lost at delivery, blood loss can be estimated by calculating 500 mL loss for each 3 volume percent drop in hematocrit.

9
00:00:54,762 --> 00:01:00,912
Its nadir depends on the speed of resuscitation with intravenous crystalloids and blood products.

10
00:01:00,912 --> 00:01:09,350
With ongoing blood loss, the real-time hematocrit is at its maximum whenever measured in the delivery, operating, or recovery room.

11
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:20,075
Prudently, if blood loss is considered more than average, the hematocrit is determined, and plans are made for close observation for potential physiological deterioration.

12
00:01:20,075 --> 00:01:30,712
Blood loss determination as recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) is discussed in detail in Chapter 42.

13
00:01:30,712 --> 00:01:35,650
Urine output measured hourly is one of the most important “vital signs.

14
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:44,712
Unless diuretic agents are given—and these are seldom indicated with active bleeding—accurately measured urine flow reflects renal perfusion.

15
00:01:44,712 --> 00:01:48,737
This in turn reflects perfusion of other vital organs.

16
00:01:48,737 --> 00:01:58,037
The volume of urine output should be ≥30 mL/hr and preferably ≥50 mL/hr.

17
00:01:58,037 --> 00:02:06,862
Another important factor to consider with management of hemorrhage is whether there are adequate procoagulants to achieve clot formation and stability.

18
00:02:06,862 --> 00:02:19,875
Many cases of severe obstetrical hemorrhage are further complicated by disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), in which blood has dysfunctional coagulation (p. 775).

19
00:02:19,875 --> 00:02:22,175
Management Of Hemorrhage.

20
00:02:22,175 --> 00:02:24,937
# Hypovolemic Shock.

21
00:02:24,937 --> 00:02:28,537
Shock from hemorrhage evolves through several stages .

22
00:02:28,537 --> 00:02:37,925
Early, the mean arterial pressure, stroke volume, cardiac output, central venous pressure, and pulmonary capillary wedge pressure decline.

23
00:02:37,925 --> 00:02:47,787
Greater differences in arteriovenous oxygen content values reflect enhanced tissue oxygen extraction, although overall oxygen consumption falls.

24
00:02:47,787 --> 00:02:57,287
Blood flow to capillary beds is controlled by arterioles, which are resistance vessels and partially controlled by the central nervous system (CNS).

25
00:02:57,287 --> 00:03:06,762
However, approximately 70 percent of total blood volume is contained in venules, which are passive resistance vessels controlled by humoral factors.

26
00:03:06,762 --> 00:03:16,137
Thus, the catecholamine release during hemorrhage prompts greater venular tone, and this provides an autotransfusion from this capacitance reservoir.

27
00:03:16,137 --> 00:03:25,850
This volume boost is accompanied by compensatory rises in heart rate, systemic and pulmonary vascular resistance, and myocardial contractility.

28
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:37,987
At the same time, selective, CNS-mediated arteriolar constriction or relaxation, termed autoregulation, preferentially redistributes cardiac output and blood volume.

29
00:03:37,987 --> 00:03:49,875
Thus, more blood flow is diverted to the heart, brain, and adrenal glands, whereas perfusion to the kidneys, splanchnic beds, muscles, skin, and uterus is relatively diminished.

30
00:03:49,875 --> 00:04:00,275
When the blood volume deficit exceeds approximately 25 percent, compensatory mechanisms usually are inadequate to maintain cardiac output and blood pressure.

31
00:04:00,275 --> 00:04:06,562
Importantly, additional small losses of blood will now cause rapid clinical deterioration.

32
00:04:06,562 --> 00:04:17,237
Following an initial augmented total oxygen extraction by maternal tissue, maldistribution of blood flow results in local tissue hypoxia and metabolic acidosis.

33
00:04:17,237 --> 00:04:23,225
This creates a vicious cycle of vasoconstriction, organ ischemia, and cellular death.

34
00:04:23,225 --> 00:04:31,550
Hemorrhage also activates lymphocytes and monocytes, which in turn prompts endothelial cell activation and platelet aggregation .

35
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:38,725
This endotheliopathy promotes release of vasoactive mediators that occlude small vessels and further impair

36
00:04:38,884 --> 00:04:41,671
microcirculatory perfusion.

37
00:04:41,621 --> 00:04:53,859
Comorbid preeclampsia or sepsis also leads to loss of capillary endothelial integrity, additional loss of intravascular volume into the extracellular space, and platelet aggregation.

38
00:04:53,859 --> 00:04:56,809
These then can incite DIC.

39
00:04:56,809 --> 00:05:09,071
The pathophysiological events just described create important but often overlooked extracellular fluid and electrolyte shifts involved in both the genesis and successful treatment of hypovolemic shock.

40
00:05:09,071 --> 00:05:17,684
These include changes in the cellular transport of various ions such as sodium and water into skeletal muscle as well as potassium loss.

41
00:05:17,684 --> 00:05:23,334
Replacement of extracellular fluid and intravascular volume are both necessary.

42
00:05:23,334 --> 00:05:32,221
Patient survival rates are enhanced in acute hemorrhagic shock if blood plus crystalloid solution are given compared with blood transfusions alone.

43
00:05:32,221 --> 00:05:35,034
# Fluid Resuscitation.

44
00:05:35,034 --> 00:05:43,596
Whenever excessive blood loss is suspected, steps are simultaneously taken to identify the bleeding source and to begin resuscitation.

45
00:05:43,596 --> 00:05:50,521
Refer to the algorithm for hemorrhage management (Fig. 42-3, p. 735).

46
00:05:50,521 --> 00:05:56,509
If the woman is undelivered, restoration of blood volume benefits both mother and fetus.

47
00:05:56,509 --> 00:05:59,646
It also prepares for emergent delivery.

48
00:05:59,646 --> 00:06:08,646
If she is postpartum, immediately identifying uterine atony, retained placental fragments, or genital tract lacerations is essential.

49
00:06:08,646 --> 00:06:18,771
One or two large-bore intravenous infusion systems are ideally established promptly, crystalloid solutions are rapidly infused, and blood products are ordered.

50
00:06:18,771 --> 00:06:24,521
An operating room is readied, and a surgical and anesthesia team are quickly assembled.

51
00:06:24,521 --> 00:06:29,359
Specific management of hemorrhage is further dependent on its etiology.

52
00:06:29,359 --> 00:06:34,471
A neonatal resuscitation team is included if imminent delivery is planned.

53
00:06:34,471 --> 00:06:41,871
Serious hemorrhage demands prompt and adequate refilling of the intravascular compartment with crystalloid solutions.

54
00:06:41,871 --> 00:06:52,171
These rapidly equilibrate into the extravascular space, and only 20 percent of crystalloid remains intravascular in critically ill patients after 1 hour .

55
00:06:52,171 --> 00:06:58,759
Because of this, initial fluid is infused in a volume two to three times the estimated blood loss.

56
00:06:58,759 --> 00:07:07,171
Crystalloid Versus Colloid Solutions Resuscitation of hypovolemic shock with colloid versus crystalloid solutions has been debated.

57
00:07:07,171 --> 00:07:20,959
In a Cochrane review of resuscitation of nonpregnant critically ill patients, Lewis and coworkers (2018) found selection of colloids versus crystalloids probably makes little or no difference to mortality rates.

58
00:07:20,959 --> 00:07:30,884
Similar results were found in the Saline versus Albumin Fluid Evaluation (SAFE) randomized trial of almost 7000 nonpregnant patients .

59
00:07:30,884 --> 00:07:37,796
At Parkland Hospital, acute volume resuscitation is preferably done with crystalloid solutions and blood.

60
00:07:37,796 --> 00:07:42,796
Either a saline-based or a balanced crystalloid solution may be infused.

61
00:07:42,796 --> 00:07:50,271
The latter includes Ringer lactate and Plasma-Lyte solutions, which have electrolyte compositions similar to plasma.

62
00:07:50,271 --> 00:08:00,909
In some studies, but not in all, balanced crystalloid solutions were found to be superior to saline-based ones (Semler, 2018; Yule, 2020).

63
00:08:00,909 --> 00:08:07,371
Excessive saline-based crystalloid solution can theoretically lead to hyperchloremic acidosis.

64
00:08:07,371 --> 00:08:15,471
In comparisons, the difficulty lies in separating the effects of the underlying pathophysiology driving the acidosis.

65
00:08:15,471 --> 00:08:17,996
# Blood Replacement.

66
00:08:17,996 --> 00:08:26,359
Type and Screen Versus Crossmatch A blood type and antibody screen should be performed for any woman at significant risk for hemorrhage.

67
00:08:26,359 --> 00:08:35,446
Because prediction of women at risk for postpartum hemorrhage is poor, we perform type and screen in all women upon admission to labor and delivery.

68
00:08:35,446 --> 00:08:45,296
Screening involves mixing maternal serum with standard reagent red cells that carry antigens to which most of the common clinically significant antibodies react.

69
00:08:45,296 --> 00:08:52,596
Instead, crossmatching involves the use of actual donor erythrocytes rather than the standardized red cells.

70
00:08:52,596 --> 00:09:03,409
This process is efficient, and only 0.03 to 0.07 percent of patients identified as having no antibodies are subsequently found to them .

71
00:09:03,409 --> 00:09:09,534
Importantly, administration of screened blood rarely results in adverse clinical sequelae.

72
00:09:09,534 --> 00:09:15,971
Transfusion Thresholds The hematocrit level or hemoglobin concentration threshold that mandates

73
00:09:16,132 --> 00:09:19,069
blood transfusion is controversial .

74
00:09:19,019 --> 00:09:30,444
Cardiac output does not substantively drop until the hemoglobin concentration falls to approximately 7 g/dL or when the hematocrit approximates 20 volume percent.

75
00:09:30,444 --> 00:09:45,519
Military combat trauma units have used a target hematocrit of 21 volume percent, and at this level, most experts recommend consideration of red cell transfusion (Carson, 2017; Kogutt, 2019).

76
00:09:45,519 --> 00:09:54,494
In general, with ongoing obstetrical hemorrhage, we recommend rapid blood infusion when the hematocrit is <25 volume percent.

77
00:09:54,494 --> 00:10:07,819
Other decision-modifying factors are whether the fetus is undelivered; surgery is imminent or ongoing operative blood loss is expected; or maternal hypoxia, vascular collapse, or other factors are present.

78
00:10:07,819 --> 00:10:10,732
Limited data address these issues.

79
00:10:10,732 --> 00:10:28,444
In a study from the Canadian Critical Care Trials Group, nonpregnant patients were randomly assigned to restrictive red cell transfusions to maintain hemoglobin concentration >7 g/dL or to liberal transfusions to maintain the hemoglobin level at 10 to 12 g/dL.

80
00:10:28,444 --> 00:10:38,819
The 30-day mortality rate was similar—19 versus 23 percent—in the restrictive versus liberal groups, respectively (Hébert, 1999).

81
00:10:38,819 --> 00:10:50,732
Transfusion therapy in nonpregnant patients with septic shock had similar mortality rates when 7 g/dL was compared with 9 g/dL as targets for transfusions .

82
00:10:50,732 --> 00:10:59,944
The number of units transfused in a given woman to reach a target hematocrit depends on her body mass and on the expectations of additional blood loss.

83
00:10:59,944 --> 00:11:08,082
Blood Component Products The content and effects of transfusion of various blood components are shown in Table 44-1.

84
00:11:08,082 --> 00:11:19,219
Because whole blood is rarely available, most women with obstetrical hemorrhage and ongoing massive blood loss are given packed red cells, crystalloid solutions, and blood components.

85
00:11:19,219 --> 00:11:30,069
As subsequently discussed, many institutions use massive transfusion protocols (MTPs) designed to anticipate all facets of massive obstetrical hemorrhage.

86
00:11:30,069 --> 00:11:40,782
These protocols commonly contain plasma, cryoprecipitate, and platelets in various ratios (Cunningham, 2015; Shields, 2011).

87
00:11:40,782 --> 00:11:43,282
Table 44-1.

88
00:11:43,282 --> 00:11:59,757
Blood Products Commonly Transfused in Obstetrical Hemorrhage Several studies have assessed plasma:red cell ratios with MTPs used in civilian trauma units and military combat hospitals (Hardin, 2014; Rahouma, 2018).

89
00:11:59,757 --> 00:12:17,619
Patients receiving a massive transfusion—defined as 10 or more units of blood—had much higher survival rates as the ratio of plasma to red cell units neared 1:1.4, that is, one unit of plasma given for each 1.4 units of packed red cells.

90
00:12:17,619 --> 00:12:23,007
By way of contrast, the highest mortality group had a ratio of 1:8.

91
00:12:23,007 --> 00:12:38,394
From the foregoing, when red cell replacement exceeds five units or so, evaluation of platelet count, clotting studies, and plasma fibrinogen concentration is reasonable (Bienstock, 2021; Pacheco, 2019).

92
00:12:38,394 --> 00:12:47,557
In the woman with obstetrical hemorrhage, the platelet count should be maintained >50,000/µL by the infusion of platelet concentrates.

93
00:12:47,557 --> 00:12:58,907
A fibrinogen level <150 mg/dL or a sufficiently prolonged PT or PTT in a woman with surgical bleeding is an indication for replacement.

94
00:12:58,907 --> 00:13:08,132
Fresh-frozen plasma is administered in doses of 10 to 15 mL/kg, or alternatively, cryoprecipitate is infused .

95
00:13:08,132 --> 00:13:13,407
At Parkland Hospital, we use a standardized response to active hemorrhage.

96
00:13:13,407 --> 00:13:28,344
If whole blood is not available, the following escalation of blood products is applied: 2 units packed red blood cells, and if continued bleeding, 2 units packed red blood cells and 2 units fresh-frozen plasma are automatically ordered.

97
00:13:28,344 --> 00:13:31,569
Beyond this, MTP is initiated.

98
00:13:31,569 --> 00:13:38,757
Whole Blood and Packed Red Blood Cells Compatible whole blood is ideal for management of severe obstetrical hemorrhage.

99
00:13:38,757 --> 00:13:47,694
It has a shelf life of 40 days, and 70 percent of the transfused red cells function for at least 24 hours following transfusion.

100
00:13:47,694 --> 00:13:52,282
One unit raises the hematocrit by 3 to 4 volume percent.

101
00:13:52,282 --> 00:14:03,119
Important for obstetrical hemorrhage, whole blood replaces many coagulation factors needed in obstetrics—especially fibrinogen—and its plasma treats hypovolemia.

102
00:14:03,119 --> 00:14:08,719
A collateral derivative is that women with severe hemorrhage are resuscitated with fewer blood

103
00:14:08,884 --> 00:14:13,059
donor exposures than with packed red cells and components.

104
00:14:13,009 --> 00:14:23,221
Experience at Parkland Hospital supports the preferable use of whole blood for massive hemorrhage (Alexander, 2009; Hernandez, 2012).

105
00:14:23,221 --> 00:14:46,471
Of more than 66,000 deliveries, women with obstetrical hemorrhage treated with whole blood had significantly lower incidences of severe maternal morbidity that included renal failure, acute respiratory distress syndrome, pulmonary edema, hypofibrinogenemia, intensive care unit admissions, or maternal death compared with those given packed red cells and component therapy.

106
00:14:46,471 --> 00:14:54,459
One unit of packed erythrocytes is derived from one unit of whole blood to have a hematocrit of 55 to 80 volume percent.

107
00:14:54,459 --> 00:15:00,821
One unit will increase the hematocrit by 3 to 4 volume percent depending on the size of the woman.

108
00:15:00,821 --> 00:15:11,484
Dilutional Coagulopathy A major drawback of massive hemorrhage treatment with crystalloid solutions and packed red blood cells is depletion of platelets and clotting factors.

109
00:15:11,484 --> 00:15:23,109
Discussed later, this can lead to a dilutional coagulopathy that is clinically indistinguishable from DIC (Cunningham, 2015; Hossain, 2013).

110
00:15:23,109 --> 00:15:30,159
Thrombocytopenia is the most frequent coagulation defect found with blood loss and multiple transfusions.

111
00:15:30,159 --> 00:15:41,021
In addition, packed red cells have only very small amounts of soluble clotting factors, and stored whole blood is deficient in platelets and in factors V, VIII, and XI.

112
00:15:41,021 --> 00:15:50,784
Thus, hypofibrinogenemia and prolongation of the prothrombin (PT) and partial thromboplastin times (PTT) are other sequelae.

113
00:15:50,784 --> 00:16:00,196
Because many causes of obstetrical hemorrhage also cause DIC, the distinction between dilutional and consumptive coagulopathy can be confusing.

114
00:16:00,196 --> 00:16:03,571
Fortunately, treatment for both is similar.

115
00:16:03,571 --> 00:16:12,596
Platelets Platelet transfusions are considered with ongoing obstetrical hemorrhage if the platelet count falls below 50,000/µL .

116
00:16:12,596 --> 00:16:20,659
In the nonsurgical patient, bleeding is rarely encountered if the platelet count is 10,000/µL or higher .

117
00:16:20,659 --> 00:16:26,084
The preferable source of platelets is one “bag” obtained by single-donor apheresis.

118
00:16:26,084 --> 00:16:31,309
This contains the equivalent of one unit each from six individual donors.

119
00:16:31,309 --> 00:16:41,096
Depending on maternal size, each singledonor-apheresis, six-unit bag raises the platelet count by approximately 20,000/µL.

120
00:16:41,096 --> 00:16:49,621
If these bags are not available, then individual-donor platelet units are used, and six to eight such units are generally transfused.

121
00:16:49,621 --> 00:16:56,246
Importantly, the donor plasma in platelet units must be compatible with recipient erythrocytes.

122
00:16:56,246 --> 00:17:06,471
Further, because some red blood cells are invariably transfused along with the platelets, only units from D-negative donors should be given to D-negative recipients.

123
00:17:06,471 --> 00:17:13,196
Fresh-frozen Plasma This component is prepared by separating plasma from whole blood and then freezing it.

124
00:17:13,196 --> 00:17:17,946
Approximately 30 minutes are required for frozen plasma to thaw.

125
00:17:17,946 --> 00:17:23,396
It is a source of all stable and labile clotting factors, including fibrinogen.

126
00:17:23,396 --> 00:17:29,546
Thus, it is often used for treatment of women with consumptive or dilutional coagulopathy.

127
00:17:29,546 --> 00:17:35,946
Plasma is not a suitable volume expander in the absence of specific clotting factor deficiencies.

128
00:17:35,946 --> 00:17:45,234
It is considered in a bleeding woman with a fibrinogen level <150 mg/dL or with an abnormal PT or PTT.

129
00:17:45,234 --> 00:17:50,059
An alternative to frozen plasma is liquid plasma (LQP).

130
00:17:50,059 --> 00:17:58,809
This neverfrozen plasma is stored at 1 to 60 C for up to 40 days, and its use compares favorably with fresh-frozen plasma .

131
00:17:58,809 --> 00:18:03,246
Liquid plasma is not universally available in many centers.

132
00:18:03,246 --> 00:18:11,309
Cryoprecipitate and Fibrinogen Concentrate Each unit of cryoprecipitate is prepared from one unit of fresh-frozen plasma.

133
00:18:11,309 --> 00:18:23,721
Each 10- to 15-mL unit contains at least 200 mg of fibrinogen along with factor VIII:C, factor VIII:von Willebrand factor, factor XIII, and fibronectin .

134
00:18:23,721 --> 00:18:31,471
It is usually given as a “pool” or “bag” using an aliquot of fibrinogen concentrate taken from 8 to 120 donors.

135
00:18:31,471 --> 00:18:38,759
Cryoprecipitate is an ideal source of fibrinogen when levels are dangerously low and surgical incisions show oozing.

136
00:18:38,759 --> 00:18:48,084
Because of transit time, the return of cryoprecipitate to transfusion services is often not feasible and is considered wastage if not deployed.

137
00:18:48,084 --> 00:18:50,946
Nearly 17 percent of ordered

138
00:18:51,100 --> 00:18:58,825
cryoprecipitate has been reported to be wasted in labor and delivery units, and this is the most commonly wasted product .

139
00:18:58,775 --> 00:19:03,562
Another alternative is virus-inactivated fibrinogen concentrate .

140
00:19:03,562 --> 00:19:09,012
These pooled fibrinogen products are marketed as RiaSTAP and Fibryna.

141
00:19:09,012 --> 00:19:15,875
Each gram of concentrate raises the plasma fibrinogen level approximately 40 mg/dL.

142
00:19:15,875 --> 00:19:21,250
These concentrates are used to treat congenital hypofibrinogenemia syndromes.

143
00:19:21,250 --> 00:19:28,737
Recombinant Activated Factor VII This synthetic vitamin K–dependent protein is available as NovoSeven.

144
00:19:28,737 --> 00:19:40,725
Recombinant activated factor VII (rFVIIa) binds to exposed tissue factor at the injury site to generate thrombin that activates platelets and the coagulation cascade.

145
00:19:40,725 --> 00:19:53,300
Since its introduction, rFVIIa has been used to help control hemorrhage from surgery, trauma, and obstetrical causes (Goodnough, 2016; Murakami, 2015).

146
00:19:53,300 --> 00:20:08,325
Many Level I trauma centers include it in MTPs. Importantly, rFVIIa will not be effective if the plasma fibrinogen level is <50 mg/dL or the platelet count is <30,000/µL.

147
00:20:08,325 --> 00:20:15,825
One concern with rFVIIa is associated arterial—and to a lesser degree— venous thrombosis.

148
00:20:15,825 --> 00:20:23,012
In a review of 35 randomized trials, arterial thromboembolism developed in 55 percent .

149
00:20:23,012 --> 00:20:27,812
A second concern is that it has been found to have only marginal efficacy .

150
00:20:27,812 --> 00:20:35,825
Tranexamic Acid Normally, plasminogen binds with tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) to form plasmin.

151
00:20:35,825 --> 00:20:42,587
This binding degrades fibrin into fibrinogen–fibrin degradation products and leads to clot lysis.

152
00:20:42,587 --> 00:20:51,200
Instead, tranexamic acid (TXA) reversibly binds to plasminogen, and thereby blocks plasmin binding to fibrin.

153
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:56,262
Fibrin strands are not broken, and a clot persists to slow down bleeding.

154
00:20:56,262 --> 00:21:06,800
Ahmadzia and coworkers (2021) recently reported the pharmacodynamics of TXA at cesarean delivery using rotational thromboelastometry.

155
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:11,937
TXA inhibited tPA-induced clot lysis in a dosedependent manner.

156
00:21:11,937 --> 00:21:27,662
In the randomized WOMAN trial of gravidas with hemorrhage following vaginal birth or during cesarean delivery, mortality rates from obstetrical hemorrhage were 1.2 percent in those given a 1-g intravenous TXA dose plus traditional care for bleeding.

157
00:21:27,662 --> 00:21:34,575
This rate was statistically lower than the 1.7-percent death rate in women given traditional care alone .

158
00:21:34,575 --> 00:21:44,050
The trial was carried out in developing nations and because of the results, the drug is recommended by the World Health Organization (2017).

159
00:21:44,050 --> 00:21:54,137
In a large randomized trial of tranexamic acid given for prophylaxis, the drug did not decrease the rate of postpartum hemorrhage following vaginal delivery .

160
00:21:54,137 --> 00:22:09,775
In the Tranexamic Acid for Preventing Postpartum Hemorrhage Following a Cesarean Delivery—TRAAP2 trial—women undergoing cesarean delivery who were given tranexamic acid prophylaxis had a lower incidence of redcell transfusions .

161
00:22:09,775 --> 00:22:19,725
Gayet-Ageron and coworkers (2018) conducted a metaanalysis with more than 1000 patients with postpartum hemorrhage treated with TXA.

162
00:22:19,725 --> 00:22:27,275
They also analyzed data from two randomized trials that included more than 40,000 women treated with TXA.

163
00:22:27,275 --> 00:22:33,825
They concluded that treatment must be given soon after bleeding onset to reduce maternal mortality rates.

164
00:22:33,825 --> 00:22:43,912
Shakur and colleagues (2018) performed a Cochrane database review and also concluded that early TXA administration decreases maternal deaths.

165
00:22:43,912 --> 00:22:58,725
Most of these studies were conducted in impoverished countries, and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) does not recommend TXA for either prophylaxis or first-line treatment of obstetrical hemorrhage.

166
00:22:58,725 --> 00:23:03,762
If used, administration within 3 hours of delivery is recommended.

167
00:23:03,762 --> 00:23:13,487
Massive Transfusion Protocols As noted, these protocols are initiated once four to five units of packed red cells have been given within 2 hours or so.

168
00:23:13,487 --> 00:23:21,175
These protocols speed blood product delivery to provide early resuscitation and help avoid dilutional coagulopathy.

169
00:23:21,175 --> 00:23:28,337
Once activated, packed red cells, plasma, platelets, and fibrinogen are given set ratios .

170
00:23:28,337 --> 00:23:35,312
Some protocols include rFVIIa, TXA, or prothrombin complex concentrates .

171
00:23:35,312 --> 00:23:37,850
Table 44-2.

172
00:23:37,850 --> 00:23:45,387
Parkland Hospital Obstetrical Massive Transfusion Protocol The data supporting the superiority of MTPs compared

173
00:23:45,556 --> 00:23:51,868
with traditional component replacement to improve survival rates in obstetrical studies are limited.

174
00:23:51,818 --> 00:24:03,293
Most reports describe nonpregnant trauma victims, but some observational studies address obstetrical hemorrhage (Green, 2016; Pacheco, 2016).

175
00:24:03,293 --> 00:24:15,881
Viscoelastic Assays Thromboelastography (TEG) and rotational thromboelastometry (ROTEM) are point-of-care tests that assess coagulation in whole blood during massive transfusions.

176
00:24:15,881 --> 00:24:22,968
These tests work by analyzing both clot formation and breakdown in a whole blood sample from a given patient.

177
00:24:22,968 --> 00:24:31,418
Testing produces a profile of coagulation dynamics, and displayed values indicate the speed and quality of clot formation .

178
00:24:31,418 --> 00:24:38,381
These assays provide information regarding time to clot formation, clot strength, and fibrinolysis .

179
00:24:38,381 --> 00:24:45,618
Currently, they guide blood product replacement in trauma, liver transplant, and cardiac surgery patients.

180
00:24:45,618 --> 00:25:02,393
Studies of TEG and ROTEM techniques in pregnant women have confirmed the hypercoagulable state of pregnancy and provide reference ranges for use in this population (Lee, 2021; McNamara, 2019; Pacheco, 2019).

181
00:25:02,393 --> 00:25:04,981
Figure 44-1.

182
00:25:04,981 --> 00:25:08,431
Normal thromboelastograph (TEG).

183
00:25:08,431 --> 00:25:12,681
R (reaction time: start of test to clot forming.

184
00:25:12,681 --> 00:25:17,156
K (clotting time): from start to 20 mm amplitude.

185
00:25:17,156 --> 00:25:21,518
Alpha (α) angle: speed of fibrin formation.

186
00:25:21,518 --> 00:25:26,118
A15, A20: amplitude at 15 and 20 minutes.

187
00:25:26,118 --> 00:25:29,443
MCF: maximum clot firmness.

188
00:25:29,443 --> 00:25:33,468
LY30: amount of clot lysed at 30 minutes.

189
00:25:33,468 --> 00:25:37,531
Although promising, they also have several limitations.

190
00:25:37,531 --> 00:25:45,556
For example, they are less informative in a woman with torrential hemorrhage in whom clotting function would need to be measured minute-by-minute.

191
00:25:45,556 --> 00:25:50,081
They cannot be used to detect disorders of primary hemostasis .

192
00:25:50,081 --> 00:25:56,818
A major drawback is the risk of misinterpretation when tests are used by inadequately trained personnel.

193
00:25:56,818 --> 00:26:05,481
Although use is gaining favor, we and others recommend further study before these tests are widely applied for obstetrical hemorrhage treatment .

194
00:26:05,481 --> 00:26:14,606
We have found this testing modality to be more applicable after initial management of hemorrhage and with intensive care recovery than during acute hemorrhage.

195
00:26:14,606 --> 00:26:24,143
Cell Salvage and Autologous Transfusion Preoperative patient phlebotomy and autologous blood storage for transfusion in obstetrics has been disappointing.

196
00:26:24,143 --> 00:26:33,131
Exceptions are women with a rare blood type or with unusual antibodies (Pacheco, 2013; Sullivan, 2019).

197
00:26:33,131 --> 00:26:39,668
Intraoperative blood salvage with reinfusion is considered a safe intervention in obstetrical patients.

198
00:26:39,668 --> 00:26:43,431
This practice may aid women declining transfusion .

199
00:26:43,431 --> 00:26:52,693
Prior concern centered on amnionic fluid contamination and embolism (Dhariwal, 2014; Goucher, 2015).

200
00:26:52,693 --> 00:27:04,093
To evaluate benefits, one trial randomly assigned 3028 women at risk for hemorrhage and undergoing cesarean delivery either to routine care or to cell salvage.

201
00:27:04,093 --> 00:27:13,543
The rate of nonautologous donor blood transfusion was not significantly reduced in the cell salvage group—2.5 versus 3.5 percent .

202
00:27:13,543 --> 00:27:26,193
In another study, Sullivan and colleagues (2019) routinely set up most cesarean deliveries for autologous transfusions and reported a lower rate of nonautologous donor transfusion.

203
00:27:26,193 --> 00:27:32,956
Similar to prior reports, no cases of amnionic fluid embolism were reported in these two studies.

204
00:27:32,956 --> 00:27:36,131
# Transfusion Complications.

205
00:27:36,131 --> 00:27:43,318
Of serious known risks, transfusion of an incompatible blood component may result in acute hemolysis.

206
00:27:43,318 --> 00:27:48,606
If severe, this can cause DIC, acute kidney injury, and death.

207
00:27:48,606 --> 00:27:59,118
Preventable errors responsible for most of these reactions frequently include mislabeling of a specimen or incorrectly transfusing a patient not slated for those products.

208
00:27:59,118 --> 00:28:07,918
The rate of such errors in the United States is estimated to be 1 case in 14,000 units, but these events are likely underreported .

209
00:28:07,918 --> 00:28:19,543
A transfusion reaction is characterized by fever, hypotension, tachycardia, dyspnea, chest or back pain, flushing, severe anxiety, and hemoglobinuria.

210
00:28:19,543 --> 00:28:29,843
Immediate supportive measures include stopping the transfusion, treating hypotension and hyperkalemia, provoking diuresis, and alkalinizing the urine.

211
00:28:29,843 --> 00:28:40,631
Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) and transfusionassociated circulatory overload (TACO) are the most common causes of transfusion-related

212
00:28:40,780 --> 00:28:42,555
mortality .

213
00:28:42,505 --> 00:28:51,542
Affected women characteristically have severe dyspnea, hypoxia, and pulmonary edema that develop within 6 hours of transfusion .

214
00:28:51,542 --> 00:28:56,542
Of the two, TACO is more common, and its incidence nears 1 percent.

215
00:28:56,542 --> 00:29:01,817
TRALI is estimated to complicate at least 1 in 12,000 transfusions .

216
00:29:01,817 --> 00:29:16,205
Although their pathogenesis is incompletely understood, injury to the pulmonary capillaries may arise from human leukocyte antigen (HLA) antibodies and human neutrophil antibodies (HNA) in donor plasma .

217
00:29:16,205 --> 00:29:20,555
Management is supportive and may include mechanical ventilation .

218
00:29:20,555 --> 00:29:29,242
Bacterial infection from transfusion of a contaminated blood component is unusual because organism growth is discouraged by refrigeration.

219
00:29:29,242 --> 00:29:38,830
The most often implicated contaminants of red cells include Yersinia, Pseudomonas, Serratia, Acinetobacter, and Escherichia species.

220
00:29:38,830 --> 00:29:45,230
The more important risk is from bacterial contamination of platelets, which are stored at room temperature.

221
00:29:45,230 --> 00:29:50,930
Current estimates are that 1 in 1000 to 2000 platelet units are contaminated.

222
00:29:50,930 --> 00:30:02,155
Death from transfusion-related sepsis is 1 case in 17,000 transfused single-donor platelets and 1 case in 61,000 transfused apheresis-donor packs .

223
00:30:02,155 --> 00:30:06,455
Viral infection risks from transfusion have been curtailed.

224
00:30:06,455 --> 00:30:17,930
The estimated risk of human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) or of hepatitis C virus infection in screened blood is 1 case in 1 to 2 million transfused units .

225
00:30:17,930 --> 00:30:24,480
The estimated hepatitis B transmission rate is <1 case in 100,000 transfused units .

226
00:30:24,480 --> 00:30:31,417
Because of the high prevalence of the virus, cytomegalovirus-infected leukocytes are often transfused.

227
00:30:31,417 --> 00:30:47,042
Risks for transmitting West Nile virus, parvovirus B19, human Tlymphotropic virus type 1, and toxoplasmosis are slight (American Association of Blood Banks, 2013; Foroutan-Rad, 2016).

228
00:30:47,042 --> 00:30:53,392
Although rare, Zika virus has emerged as another relevant transfusion-transmitted infection .

229
00:30:53,392 --> 00:30:58,617
Collection of all whole blood components now includes testing for Zika virus .

230
00:30:58,617 --> 00:31:05,480
At this time, transmission of SARS-CoV-2 virus from a COVID-positive donor is hypothetical .

231
00:31:05,480 --> 00:31:08,367
Obstetrical Coagulopathies.

232
00:31:08,367 --> 00:31:20,467
The terms consumptive coagulopathy, defibrination syndrome, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) are often used interchangeably, but distinctions are important.

233
00:31:20,467 --> 00:31:27,567
Actual consumption of procoagulants within the intravascular tree results in a consumptive coagulopathy.

234
00:31:27,567 --> 00:31:34,392
In contrast, massive loss of procoagulants from hemorrhage results in a dilutional coagulopathy.

235
00:31:34,392 --> 00:31:44,055
Semantics aside, consumptive coagulopathy culminates in a systemic intravascular activation that completely disrupts natural hemostasis.

236
00:31:44,055 --> 00:31:53,530
As a result, an ineffective balance of natural anticoagulant mechanisms leads to widespread fibrin deposition that can cause multiorgan failure .

237
00:31:53,530 --> 00:31:57,417
# Pregnancy-induced Coagulation Changes.

238
00:31:57,417 --> 00:32:05,017
During normal pregnancy, the balance between coagulation and fibrinolysis changes to create a procoagulant state.

239
00:32:05,017 --> 00:32:16,030
Changes that promote coagulation include appreciable elevation in the plasma concentrations of factors I (fibrinogen), VII, VII, IX, and X.

240
00:32:16,030 --> 00:32:21,817
A partial list of these normal values is found in the Appendix (p. 1228).

241
00:32:21,817 --> 00:32:27,580
Concurrently, levels of plasminogen, which lyses fibrin, rise considerably.

242
00:32:27,580 --> 00:32:35,830
However, plasminogen activator inhibitor 1 and 2 (PAI-1 and PAI-2) levels also increase.

243
00:32:35,830 --> 00:32:44,242
Thus, plasmin activity usually declines until after delivery (Hale, 2012; Hui, 2012).

244
00:32:44,242 --> 00:32:57,555
As shown in Figure 47, (p. 62), mean platelet count drops by 10 percent during pregnancy, and platelet activation is enhanced (Kenny, 2015; Reese, 2018).

245
00:32:57,555 --> 00:33:10,180
The net results of these changes include greater levels of fibrinopeptide A, β-thromboglobulin, platelet factor 4, and fibrinogen–fibrin degradation products, which includes D-dimers.

246
00:33:10,180 --> 00:33:27,192
In addition to lower concentrations of the anticoagulant protein S, pregnancy-related hypercoagulability, and decreased fibrinolysis, there is augmented—yet compensated—intravascular coagulation that may function to maintain the uteroplacental interface.

247
00:33:27,192 --> 00:33:31,180
# Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation.

248
00:33:31,180 --> 00:33:39,755
Because of the many definitions and variable severity, the incidence of consumptive coagulopathy in gravidas varies and ranges from

249
00:33:39,916 --> 00:33:47,903
0.03 to 0.35 percent (Erez, 2014; Rattray, 2012).

250
00:33:47,853 --> 00:33:56,991
For example, some degree of significant coagulopathy is found in virtually all cases of placental abruption and amnionic fluid embolism.

251
00:33:56,991 --> 00:34:17,616
Other instances in which frequently occurring but less recognized degrees of coagulation activation can be found include sepsis; thrombotic microangiopathies; acute kidney injury; acute fatty liver; severe preeclampsia; and hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, low platelet count (HELLP) syndrome .

252
00:34:17,616 --> 00:34:25,403
When consumptive coagulopathy is severe, the likelihood of maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality rises.

253
00:34:25,403 --> 00:34:40,816
In one study of 49 cases, antecedent causes included those listed above, and 59 percent received blood transfusions, 18 percent underwent hysterectomy, 6 percent required renal dialysis, and three mothers died .

254
00:34:40,816 --> 00:34:45,341
In this series, the perinatal mortality rate was 30 percent.

255
00:34:45,341 --> 00:34:52,728
From 2010 to 2011, DIC was the second most common severe maternal morbidity indicator .

256
00:34:52,728 --> 00:34:58,428
DIC was associated with nearly a fourth of maternal deaths during this study period.

257
00:34:58,428 --> 00:35:10,478
Despite these statistics, consumptive coagulopathy as the sole cause of maternal death is uncommon and accounts for only 0.2 percent of pregnancyrelated deaths in the United States .

258
00:35:10,478 --> 00:35:14,053
# Activation of Normal Coagulation.

259
00:35:14,053 --> 00:35:26,903
Instead of the “waterfall” sequential activation of the clotting cascade, a current theory proposes that tissue factor—an integral membrane glycoprotein—serves as the principal initiator of coagulation .

260
00:35:26,903 --> 00:35:31,578
Coagulation then moves forward but incorporates a feedback loop.

261
00:35:31,578 --> 00:35:54,403
To begin, tissue factor forms complexes with factor VII/VIIa to activate factors IX and X. Tissue factor is found in highly vascularized organs such as the brain, lungs, and placenta; in amnionic fluid; and in certain other cells (Kuczyfiski, 2002; Østerud, 2006; Uszyfiski, 2001).

262
00:35:54,403 --> 00:36:02,778
Tissue factor–factor VIIa complexes ultimately generate activated factor X (Xa) to initiate clotting.

263
00:36:02,778 --> 00:36:08,628
Subsequently, the previously labeled “intrinsic” pathway amplifies this process.

264
00:36:08,628 --> 00:36:16,503
Specifically, the initial thrombin produced directly activates factor XI by providing a feedback amplification loop .

265
00:36:16,503 --> 00:36:21,778
The end result of this amplified coagulation process is fibrin formation.

266
00:36:21,778 --> 00:36:27,766
This is then counterbalanced by the fibrinolytic system, in which plasminogen is activated.

267
00:36:27,766 --> 00:36:31,691
Even this process is tied initially to tissue factor.

268
00:36:31,691 --> 00:36:39,503
The final product is fibrin degradation products, sometimes called fibrin split products, which include D-dimers.

269
00:36:39,503 --> 00:36:42,103
Figure 44-2.

270
00:36:42,103 --> 00:36:45,078
Coagulation pathway schematic.

271
00:36:45,078 --> 00:36:55,216
FSP = fibrin split products; PAI = plasminogen activator inhibitor; TFPI = tissue factor pathway inhibitor.

272
00:36:55,216 --> 00:36:59,278
# Activation of Pathological Coagulation.

273
00:36:59,278 --> 00:37:10,928
With DIC, pathological entities prompt tissue factor release from subendothelial tissue and stimulated monocytes, which in turn provoke cytokine release from the endothelium.

274
00:37:10,928 --> 00:37:17,253
With generalized endothelial activation, diffuse activation of coagulation follows.

275
00:37:17,253 --> 00:37:28,941
This pathological cycle of coagulation and fibrinolysis becomes clinically important when coagulation factors and platelets are sufficiently depleted to create consumptive coagulopathy.

276
00:37:28,941 --> 00:37:34,978
Purpura fulminans is a severe—often lethal—form of consumptive coagulopathy.

277
00:37:34,978 --> 00:37:41,966
It is caused by microthrombi in small blood vessels leading to skin necrosis and sometimes vasculitis.

278
00:37:41,966 --> 00:37:49,241
Debridement of large areas of skin over the extremities and buttocks frequently requires treatment in a burn unit.

279
00:37:49,241 --> 00:37:53,166
Rarely, four-extremity amputation is required .

280
00:37:53,166 --> 00:38:05,953
Purpura fulminans usually complicates sepsis in women with heterozygous protein C deficiencies and low protein C serum levels (Bhatti, 2019; Levi, 2016).

281
00:38:05,953 --> 00:38:13,766
Importantly, homozygous protein C or S deficiency results in neonatal purpura fulminans, which is fatal .

282
00:38:13,766 --> 00:38:16,491
# Inciting Conditions.

283
00:38:16,491 --> 00:38:21,303
Several obstetrical syndromes can trigger consumptive coagulopathy.

284
00:38:21,303 --> 00:38:32,666
Of these, placental abruption is the most common cause of severe consumptive coagulopathy in obstetrics and is discussed more fully in Chapter 43 (p. 749).

285
00:38:32,666 --> 00:38:39,991
With preeclampsia, eclampsia, and HELLP syndrome, endothelial activation is a hallmark.

286
00:38:39,991 --> 00:38:44,103
Perhaps 10 percent of HELLP cases have some

287
00:38:44,260 --> 00:38:46,897
disseminated coagulation .

288
00:38:46,847 --> 00:38:58,785
In general, the clinical severity of preeclampsia is directly correlated with thrombocytopenia and fibrin degradation products (Gedik 2017; Kenny, 2015).

289
00:38:58,785 --> 00:39:04,972
That said, intravascular coagulation is seldom severe enough to be clinically worrisome .

290
00:39:04,972 --> 00:39:13,035
Acute fatty liver of pregnancy also causes intravascular coagulation in addition to decreased procoagulant synthesis .

291
00:39:13,035 --> 00:39:20,435
Amnionic fluid embolism is usually caused by intravenous embolization of meconium-laden amnionic fluid.

292
00:39:20,435 --> 00:39:26,572
It results in rapid cardiorespiratory collapse and profound consumptive coagulopathy.

293
00:39:26,572 --> 00:39:35,660
The Society for MaternalFetal Medicine (2021) has developed a checklist for the initial management of amnionic fluid embolism.

294
00:39:35,660 --> 00:39:40,272
It is discussed in Chapter 42 (p. 745).

295
00:39:40,272 --> 00:39:46,197
Sepsis stemming from various infections can be accompanied by endo-or exotoxin release.

296
00:39:46,197 --> 00:39:55,110
Although a feature of sepsis syndrome includes activation of coagulation, seldom does sepsis alone cause massive procoagulant consumption.

297
00:39:55,110 --> 00:40:06,810
Escherichia coli bacteremia is frequently seen with antepartum pyelonephritis and puerperal infections, however, accompanying consumptive coagulopathy is usually not severe.

298
00:40:06,810 --> 00:40:24,235
Some notable exceptions are septicemia caused by exotoxins released from group A Streptococcus pyogenes, Staphylococcus aureus, or Clostridium perfringens, C sordellii, or C novyi (Herrera, 2016; Pritchard, 1971).

299
00:40:24,235 --> 00:40:30,147
Treatment of sepsis and septic shock is discussed in Chapter 50 (p. 889).

300
00:40:30,147 --> 00:40:37,572
Septic abortion—especially associated with these organisms—can incite coagulation and worsen hemorrhage.

301
00:40:37,572 --> 00:40:44,985
Second-trimester induced abortions can stimulate intravascular coagulation even in the absence of sepsis.

302
00:40:44,985 --> 00:40:56,522
Ben-Ami and associates (2012) described a 1.6-percent incidence in 1249 late-second-trimester pregnancies terminated by dilation and evacuation.

303
00:40:56,522 --> 00:41:02,185
Two thirds were done for fetal demise, which may have contributed to coagulopathy .

304
00:41:02,185 --> 00:41:09,797
Another source of intense coagulation is instillation of hypertonic solutions to effect midtrimester abortions.

305
00:41:09,797 --> 00:41:14,260
These are not commonly used currently for pregnancy terminations.

306
00:41:14,260 --> 00:41:26,597
The mechanism is thought to initiate coagulation by thromboplastin release into maternal circulation from the placenta, fetus, and decidua by the necrobiotic effect of hypertonic solutions.

307
00:41:26,597 --> 00:41:32,622
Prolonged retention of a dead fetus is an unusual cause of consumptive coagulopathy today.

308
00:41:32,622 --> 00:41:39,660
Namely, fetal death can be easily confirmed, and highly effective methods for labor induction are available.

309
00:41:39,660 --> 00:41:54,522
Moreover, if the dead fetus is undelivered, most women enter spontaneous labor within 2 weeks and gross disruption of maternal coagulation rarely develops before 4 weeks (Pritchard, 1959, 1973).

310
00:41:54,522 --> 00:42:00,197
After 1 month, however, almost a fourth will develop consumptive coagulopathy.

311
00:42:00,197 --> 00:42:02,597
# Diagnosis.

312
00:42:02,597 --> 00:42:08,960
Bioassay is an excellent method to detect or suspect clinically significant coagulopathy.

313
00:42:08,960 --> 00:42:14,485
Excessive bleeding at sites of modest trauma characterizes defective hemostasis.

314
00:42:14,485 --> 00:42:27,672
Examples include persistent bleeding from venipuncture sites, nicks from preoperative shaving, trauma from bladder catheterization, and spontaneous bleeding from the gums, nose, or gastrointestinal tract.

315
00:42:27,672 --> 00:42:35,747
Purpura or petechiae at pressure sites such as sphygmomanometer cuffs or tourniquets suggest significant thrombocytopenia.

316
00:42:35,747 --> 00:42:50,335
Any surgical procedure provides the ultimate bioassay and elicits generalized oozing from abdominal wall layers, the retroperitoneal space, the episiotomy, or incisions and dissections for cesarean delivery or hysterectomy.

317
00:42:50,335 --> 00:42:55,635
Of laboratory tests, fibrinogen and D-dimer levels can be informative.

318
00:42:55,635 --> 00:43:03,372
In late pregnancy, plasma fibrinogen levels typically have risen to 300 to 600 mg/dL.

319
00:43:03,372 --> 00:43:13,385
Thus, even with severe consumptive coagulopathy, levels may sometimes be sufficiently high to protect against clinically harmful hypofibrinogenemia.

320
00:43:13,385 --> 00:43:24,922
For example, defibrination caused by a placental abruption might lower an initial fibrinogen level of 600 mg/dL to 250 mg/dL.

321
00:43:24,922 --> 00:43:32,497
Although this would indicate massive fibrinogen consumption, levels are still adequate to promote clinical coagulation.

322
00:43:32,497 --> 00:43:38,935
The clinically impactful threshold usually approximates 150 mg/dL.

323
00:43:38,935 --> 00:43:40,822
The effects of

324
00:43:40,972 --> 00:43:49,359
serious hypofibrinogenemia —less than 50 mg/dL—can be illustrated with clinical thrombin clot test.

325
00:43:49,309 --> 00:43:57,934
With low fibrinogen levels, the clot formed from whole blood in a glass phlebotomy tube will initially be soft and cannot retract in volume.

326
00:43:57,934 --> 00:44:05,759
Instead, during the next half hour, platelet-induced clot retraction ensues, and this causes a tighter clot to form.

327
00:44:05,759 --> 00:44:15,822
As depicted in Figure 44-2, fibrinolysis cleaves fibrin into various fibrin degradation products, and one of the smaller ones is the D-dimer.

328
00:44:15,822 --> 00:44:21,309
Several sensitive assays contain monoclonal antibodies specific for D-dimers .

329
00:44:21,309 --> 00:44:28,059
Assay values are almost always abnormally high with clinically significant consumptive coagulopathy.

330
00:44:28,059 --> 00:44:33,747
At least in obstetrical disorders, quantification does not correlate with outcomes.

331
00:44:33,747 --> 00:44:42,897
Examples of the magnitude of fibrin split product elevations in various obstetrical coagulopathies are shown in Figure 44-3.

332
00:44:42,897 --> 00:44:45,534
Figure 44-3.

333
00:44:45,534 --> 00:44:53,659
Quantification of fibrin split products in various obstetrical syndromes that cause disseminated intravascular coagulation.

334
00:44:53,659 --> 00:45:03,222
AFLP = acute fatty liver of pregnancy; HELLP = hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, low platelet count.

335
00:45:03,222 --> 00:45:12,084
Reproduced with permission from Cunningham FG, Nelson DB: Disseminated intravascular coagulation syndromes in obstetrics.

336
00:45:12,084 --> 00:45:26,122
Obstet Gynecol. 2015 Nov;126(5):999–1011) Thrombocytopenia is likely if petechiae are abundant or if clotted blood fails to retract within an hour or so.

337
00:45:26,122 --> 00:45:29,772
Confirmation is provided by a low platelet count.

338
00:45:29,772 --> 00:45:36,272
If severe preeclampsia syndrome is comorbid, qualitative platelet dysfunction may coexist .

339
00:45:36,272 --> 00:45:44,072
Prothrombin time (PT) and partial thromboplastin time (PTT) are standard coagulation tests.

340
00:45:44,072 --> 00:45:58,322
Prolongation may stem from very low fibrinogen concentrations, from appreciably reduced levels of the procoagulants needed to generate thrombin, or from large amounts of circulating fibrinogen–fibrin degradation products.

341
00:45:58,322 --> 00:46:10,497
Thromboelastometry and thromboelastography are point-of-care tests used as adjuncts to conventional laboratory studies (McNamara, 2019; Pacheco, 2019).

342
00:46:10,497 --> 00:46:17,772
Their current role may serve to guide blood product replacements as discussed earlier (p. 774).

343
00:46:17,772 --> 00:46:25,297
Using many of these tests, several organizations have attempted to establish a more uniform definition of DIC.

344
00:46:25,297 --> 00:46:31,784
One is the International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis (ISTH) scoring system.

345
00:46:31,784 --> 00:46:41,734
The score is used only after a condition known to cause intravascular coagulation is identified and is calculated using a combination of laboratory tests.

346
00:46:41,734 --> 00:46:51,447
Composite ISTH-DIC scores <5 suggest nonovert DIC, whereas scores ≥5 are compatible with overt DIC.

347
00:46:51,447 --> 00:46:58,509
A scoring system for nonovert DIC has been developed by Alhousseini and coworkers (2020).

348
00:46:58,509 --> 00:47:08,959
These scoring systems have not been applied widely in obstetrics (Hizkiyahu, 2019; Jonard, 2016; Nelson, 2014).

349
00:47:08,959 --> 00:47:11,534
# General Management.

350
00:47:11,534 --> 00:47:17,834
To halt ongoing defibrination, identifying and removing the inciting source is a priority.

351
00:47:17,834 --> 00:47:26,584
With incisions or extensive lacerations accompanied by severe hemorrhage, rapid replacement of procoagulants is usually indicated.

352
00:47:26,584 --> 00:47:33,322
Vigorous restoration and maintenance of the circulation to treat hypovolemia cannot be overemphasized.

353
00:47:33,322 --> 00:47:47,484
Adequate perfusion restores hepatic and endothelial synthesis of procoagulants and permits prompt removal of activated coagulation factors, fibrin, and fibrin degradation products by the reticuloendothelial system.

354
00:47:47,484 --> 00:47:53,059
Aside from these fundamental steps, few other agents have proved soundly effective.

355
00:47:53,059 --> 00:47:59,247
Although its use is seemingly counterintuitive, unfractionated heparin has now been abandoned.

356
00:47:59,247 --> 00:48:15,684
Other agents not recommended for first-line use include tranexamic acid or epsilonaminocaproic acid, both antifibrinolytic agents (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; Pacheco, 2017).

357
00:48:15,684 --> 00:48:25,472
This is because the fibrinolytic system is necessary for dissolution of widespread fibrin thromboses caused by generalized intravascular coagulation .

358
00:48:25,472 --> 00:48:37,072
Discussed earlier (p. 773), recombinant factor VIIa (rFVIIa) can be used to help control severe obstetrical hemorrhage from other causes.

359
00:48:37,072 --> 00:48:41,797
However, current clinical evidence is insufficient to make firm

360
00:48:41,956 --> 00:48:46,993
recommendations on its administration for obstetrical coagulopathies.

361
00:48:46,943 --> 00:48:49,681
Surgical Management Of Hemorrhage.

362
00:48:49,681 --> 00:48:54,868
Several invasive procedures can help to arrest severe postpartum hemorrhage.

363
00:48:54,868 --> 00:49:02,506
A report from the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality concluded that most studies addressing these methods are of poor quality .

364
00:49:02,506 --> 00:49:12,693
In one study of 6660 women with postpartum hemorrhage, 4 percent underwent an invasive procedure, and 1 percent had a hysterectomy .

365
00:49:12,693 --> 00:49:18,406
The failure rate of conservative surgical and embolization procedures was 15 percent.

366
00:49:18,406 --> 00:49:28,881
Of specific methods, use of uterine balloon tamponade for treatment of atony has doubled in the past decade (Merriam, 2020; Suarez, 2020).

367
00:49:28,881 --> 00:49:33,518
It is described in Chapter 42 (p. 737).

368
00:49:33,518 --> 00:49:40,256
For low-pressure bleeding, topical hemostatic agents can be used to control persistent surgical oozing.

369
00:49:40,256 --> 00:49:44,568
These were reviewed by Stachowicz and colleagues (2020).

370
00:49:44,568 --> 00:49:50,231
Other than for cesarean hysterectomy, these are seldom used in obstetrical hemorrhage.

371
00:49:50,231 --> 00:49:53,381
# Uterine Compression Sutures.

372
00:49:53,381 --> 00:50:00,868
This surgical technique uses a no. 2 chromic suture to compress the anterior and posterior uterine walls together .

373
00:50:00,868 --> 00:50:05,668
Because they give the appearance of suspenders, they are also called braces .

374
00:50:05,668 --> 00:50:16,318
Several modifications of the B-Lynch technique have been described (Gilmandyar, 2019; Matsubara, 2013; Nelson, 2007).

375
00:50:16,318 --> 00:50:19,031
Figure 44-4.

376
00:50:19,031 --> 00:50:22,143
Uterine compression suture or “brace.

377
00:50:22,143 --> 00:50:31,481
The B-Lynch suture technique is illustrated from an anterior view of the uterus in Figures A, B, and D and a posterior view in Figure C.

378
00:50:31,481 --> 00:50:36,768
The numbers denote the sequential path of the suture and are shown in more than one figure.

379
00:50:36,768 --> 00:50:38,468
Step 1.

380
00:50:38,468 --> 00:50:44,918
Beginning below the incision, the needle pierces the lower uterine segment to enter the uterine cavity.

381
00:50:44,918 --> 00:50:46,693
Step 2.

382
00:50:46,693 --> 00:50:50,193
The needle exits the cavity above the incision.

383
00:50:50,193 --> 00:50:55,406
The suture then loops up and around the fundus to the posterior uterine surface.

384
00:50:55,406 --> 00:50:57,143
Step 3.

385
00:50:57,143 --> 00:51:02,281
The needle pierces the posterior uterine wall to reenter the uterine cavity.

386
00:51:02,281 --> 00:51:06,593
The suture then traverses to the opposite side within the cavity.

387
00:51:06,593 --> 00:51:08,406
Step 4.

388
00:51:08,406 --> 00:51:13,106
The needle exits the uterine cavity through the posterior uterine wall.

389
00:51:13,106 --> 00:51:19,043
From the back of the uterus, the suture loops up and around the fundus to the front of the uterus.

390
00:51:19,043 --> 00:51:20,881
Step 5.

391
00:51:20,881 --> 00:51:26,431
The needle pierces the myometrium above the incision to reenter the uterine cavity.

392
00:51:26,431 --> 00:51:28,343
Step 6.

393
00:51:28,343 --> 00:51:34,906
The needle exits below the incision, and the sutures at points 1 and 6 are tied below the incision.

394
00:51:34,906 --> 00:51:39,131
The hysterotomy incision is then closed in the usual fashion.

395
00:51:39,131 --> 00:51:42,731
B-Lynch sutures are mainly used to treat atony.

396
00:51:42,731 --> 00:51:49,543
These may help diminish DIC-associated bleeding, while coagulation is corrected by transfusion.

397
00:51:49,543 --> 00:51:52,593
Success rates vary by indication.

398
00:51:52,593 --> 00:52:00,056
For example, B-Lynch (2005) cited 948 cases with only 7 failures.

399
00:52:00,056 --> 00:52:09,731
Conversely, in other series, overall failure rates were 20 to 25 percent (Kaya, 2016; Kayem, 2011).

400
00:52:09,731 --> 00:52:14,256
Some unique complications can rarely follow compression sutures .

401
00:52:14,256 --> 00:52:24,893
Most involve variations of uterine ischemic necrosis with peritonitis (Gottlieb, 2008; Joshi, 2004; Treloar, 2006).

402
00:52:24,893 --> 00:52:30,856
In most cases, subsequent pregnancies are uneventful if compression sutures are used .

403
00:52:30,856 --> 00:52:43,943
A few women with B-Lynch sutures, however, developed uterine wall defects or uterine cavity synechiae (Akoury, 2008; Alouini, 2011; Ibrahim, 2013).

404
00:52:43,943 --> 00:52:46,543
# Artery Ligation.

405
00:52:46,543 --> 00:52:52,518
Ligation of arterial blood flow to the uterus can halt or diminish obstetrical hemorrhage.

406
00:52:52,518 --> 00:53:00,281
Common indications include uterine hysterotomy incision laceration, uterine atony, and abnormal placentation.

407
00:53:00,281 --> 00:53:09,118
Uterine artery ligation may be unilateral or bilateral and is used mainly for lacerations at the lateral part of a hysterotomy incision .

408
00:53:09,118 --> 00:53:14,956
In our experiences, this procedure is less helpful for other hemorrhage etiologies.

409
00:53:14,956 --> 00:53:28,243
In rare cases with torrential hemorrhage from placenta accreta syndrome, a tourniquet wrapped around the lower uterine segment may slow bleeding sufficiently while resuscitation ensues and dissection for hysterectomy is performed.

410
00:53:28,243 --> 00:53:31,018
Figure 44-5.

411
00:53:31,018 --> 00:53:33,468
Uterine artery ligation.

412
00:53:33,468 --> 00:53:41,006
The suture goes through the lateral uterine wall anteriorly, curves posteriorly, and then re-enters anteriorly.

413
00:53:41,006 --> 00:53:44,618
When tied, it encompasses the uterine artery.

414
00:53:44,618 --> 00:53:47,593
Internal iliac artery ligation

415
00:53:47,764 --> 00:53:51,001
may also be unilateral or bilateral.

416
00:53:50,951 --> 00:53:55,439
For years, ligation has been used to reduce pelvic hemorrhage.

417
00:53:55,439 --> 00:54:08,114
However, the procedure may be technically difficult and is successful in only half of cases (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019a; Bienstock, 2021).

418
00:54:08,114 --> 00:54:15,539
Wei and colleagues (2019) reported it to be successful for improved hemostasis with placenta previa.

419
00:54:15,539 --> 00:54:20,514
It is not particularly helpful for abating hemorrhage with postpartum atony .

420
00:54:20,514 --> 00:54:32,914
For internal iliac ligation, adequate exposure is obtained by opening the peritoneum over the common iliac artery and dissecting down to the bifurcation of the external and internal iliac arteries .

421
00:54:32,914 --> 00:54:40,564
Branches distal to the external iliac arteries are palpated to verify pulsations at or below the inguinal area.

422
00:54:40,564 --> 00:54:52,014
Ligation of the internal iliac artery at a point 5 cm distal to the common iliac bifurcation will usually avoid the internal iliac artery’s posterior division branches .

423
00:54:52,014 --> 00:55:01,239
The areolar sheath of the artery is incised longitudinally, and a right-angle clamp is carefully passed just beneath the artery from lateral to medial.

424
00:55:01,239 --> 00:55:07,814
Care must be taken not to perforate contiguous large veins, especially the internal iliac vein.

425
00:55:07,814 --> 00:55:15,226
Suture— usually nonabsorbable—is passed under the artery with a clamp, and the vessel is then securely ligated.

426
00:55:15,226 --> 00:55:18,014
Figure 44-6.

427
00:55:18,014 --> 00:55:21,464
Ligation of the right internal iliac artery.

428
00:55:21,464 --> 00:55:31,564
Unembalmed cadaveric dissection shows the right-angle clamp passing underneath the anterior division of the internal iliac artery just distal to its posterior division.

429
00:55:31,564 --> 00:55:35,351
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Marlene Corton.

430
00:55:35,351 --> 00:55:45,439
The most important mechanism of action with internal iliac artery ligation is an 85-percent reduction in pulse pressure in those arteries distal to the ligation .

431
00:55:45,439 --> 00:55:52,026
This converts an arterial pressure system into one with pressures approaching those in the venous circulation.

432
00:55:52,026 --> 00:55:57,601
This creates vessels more amenable to hemostasis via pressure and clot formation.

433
00:55:57,601 --> 00:56:04,539
Even bilateral internal iliac artery ligation does not appear to interfere with subsequent reproduction.

434
00:56:04,539 --> 00:56:10,839
Nizard and colleagues (2003) reported subsequent pregnancy in 17 such women.

435
00:56:10,839 --> 00:56:19,464
From a total of 21 pregnancies, 13 were normal, 3 ended with miscarriage, 3 were terminated, and 2 were ectopic.

436
00:56:19,464 --> 00:56:22,664
# Angiographic Embolization.

437
00:56:22,664 --> 00:56:29,414
This modality is now used for many causes of intractable hemorrhage when surgical access is difficult.

438
00:56:29,414 --> 00:56:33,151
Its use has increased remarkably in the past decade .

439
00:56:33,151 --> 00:56:46,976
In more than 500 pregnancy-related cases, embolization was effective in 90 percent (Grönvall, 2014; Lee, 2012; Poujade, 2011; Zhang, 2015).

440
00:56:46,976 --> 00:56:55,651
After his review, Rouse (2013) concluded that embolization can be used to arrest refractory postpartum hemorrhage.

441
00:56:55,651 --> 00:56:58,901
Other reports have been less enthusiastic.

442
00:56:58,901 --> 00:57:08,951
Fertility is not impaired, and many subsequent successful pregnancies have been reported (Fiori, 2009; Kolomeyevskaya, 2009).

443
00:57:08,951 --> 00:57:18,226
An important caveat for these procedures is that women with hemodynamic instability related to active bleeding should not be removed from the operating room.

444
00:57:18,226 --> 00:57:22,464
Complications are relatively uncommon but can be severe.

445
00:57:22,464 --> 00:57:36,914
Case reports detail instances of iatrogenic iliac artery rupture, uterine ischemic necrosis, and uterine infection (Grönvall, 2014; Katakam, 2009; Nakash, 2012).

446
00:57:36,914 --> 00:57:44,376
A rare case of massive buttock necrosis and paraplegia followed bilateral internal iliac artery embolization .

447
00:57:44,376 --> 00:57:49,326
A myometrial defect in subsequent pregnancy also has been reported .

448
00:57:49,326 --> 00:57:55,364
In a few instances, massive blood loss and difficult surgical dissection is anticipated.

449
00:57:55,364 --> 00:58:04,526
The use of balloon-tipped catheters preoperatively inserted into the iliac or uterine arteries is described in management of placenta accreta spectrum .

450
00:58:04,526 --> 00:58:07,114
# Aortic Compression.

451
00:58:07,114 --> 00:58:14,614
Prophylactic use of an aortic balloon occlusion device has been reported to reduce blood loss and the need for hysterectomy .

452
00:58:14,614 --> 00:58:28,226
Also known as resuscitative endovascular balloon occlusion of the aorta (REBOA), such occlusion lowers the perfusion pressure distally, increases cardiac afterload, and redistributes blood volume to the heart and brain .

453
00:58:28,226 --> 00:58:33,351
Prolonged occlusion can result in lower limb ischemia requiring amputation.

454
00:58:33,508 --> 00:58:42,508
Whittington and colleagues (2020) described its successful prophylactic use in 11 women with placenta accreta spectrum disorders.

455
00:58:42,458 --> 00:58:50,745
Instead, manual compression of the aorta above the sacral promontory can be used in cases in which pelvic hemorrhage is torrential.

456
00:58:50,745 --> 00:58:56,945
If the abdomen is closed, pressure placed above the umbilicus reduces blood pressure distally .

457
00:58:56,945 --> 00:58:59,370
# Pelvic Packing.

458
00:58:59,370 --> 00:59:10,970
If hysterectomy fails to curtail hemorrhage, then pelvic packing with gauze and termination of the operation may be considered (Pacheo, 2018; Touhami, 2018).

459
00:59:10,970 --> 00:59:15,020
Rolls of gauze are packed to provide constant local pressure.

460
00:59:15,020 --> 00:59:21,308
In some cases, this may serve as a temporizing step prior to interventional embolization.

461
00:59:21,308 --> 00:59:26,908
In other cases, packing alone may be left for 48 to 72 hours.

462
00:59:26,908 --> 00:59:32,145
If the patient is stable and bleeding appears to have stopped, packing is removed.

463
00:59:32,145 --> 00:59:37,770
In one review of 104 cases, the success rate was 79 percent.

464
00:59:37,770 --> 00:59:46,120
Packing failed in 24 patients, and 13 of these women died, yielding an overall mortality rate of 13 percent .

465
00:59:46,120 --> 00:59:50,295
The umbrella or parachute pack uses a similar concept .

466
00:59:50,295 --> 01:00:01,583
Although seldom used today, it can be lifesaving if all other measures have failed, especially in low-resource areas (Dildy, 2006; Touhami, 2018).

467
01:00:01,583 --> 01:00:08,895
The pack is constructed of a sturdy sterile plastic bag that is filled with gauze rolls that are unwound and knotted together.

468
01:00:08,895 --> 01:00:14,195
Sufficient rolls are used to provide adequate volume in the bag to fill the pelvis.

469
01:00:14,195 --> 01:00:19,170
The pack is introduced transabdominally with the stalk exiting the vagina.

470
01:00:19,170 --> 01:00:26,020
Mild traction is applied by tying the stalk to a 1-liter fluid bag, which is hung over the foot of the bed.

471
01:00:26,020 --> 01:00:30,408
The umbrella pack is removed vaginally after 24 hours.

